%[text] %[text:anchor:T_C6687F79] # Riemann Sums
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:../../MainMenu.m)
%[text] One of the most important and useful concepts of calculus is the idea of computing accumulation. In many situations, there is information about a rate of change (e.g., acceleration, velocity, concentration, flow, power) and calculus presents tools for calculating the accumulation that occurs (e.g., velocity, position, amount, volume, energy, etc). 
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5DD0C241] ## Riemann Sums with Rectangular Approximations
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_CD29503B] Approximation by rectangular Riemann sums is quite common.
% Initialize parameters
syms t
f(t) =  sin(t); %[control:editfield:5334]{"position":[8,15]}
a =  -pi; b =  pi;     %[control:editfield:2a29]{"position":[5,9]} %[control:editfield:4ad3]{"position":[15,18]}
% How many intervals do you want?
Intervals = 7; %[control:slider:3fd9]{"position":[13,14]}
tAllData = linspace(a,b,ceil(2000/Intervals)*Intervals+1);  % domain for plotting
fAllData = f(tAllData);         % range for plotting

dt = (b-a)/Intervals;
tData = a:dt:b;
fData = f(tData);
% Select the Riemann sum method of value approximation
EndPt = "midpoint"; %[control:dropdown:72af]{"position":[9,19]}
if EndPt == "random"
    RepPts = tData(1:end-1) + dt.*rand([1 Intervals]);
    fData = [fData f(RepPts)];
    tData = [tData RepPts];
elseif EndPt == "midpoint"
    fData = [fData f((tData(2:end)+tData(1:end-1))/2)];
elseif EndPt == "maximum"
    fMaxHelp = reshape(fAllData(1:end-1),[],Intervals);
    fMaxHelp = [fMaxHelp; fData(2:end)];
    fData = [fData max(fMaxHelp)];
elseif EndPt == "minimum"
    fMinHelp = reshape(fAllData(1:end-1),[],Intervals);
    fMinHelp = [fMinHelp; fData(2:end)];
    fData = [fData min(fMinHelp)];
end
  %[control:button:6fef]{"position":[1,2]}
% Graph the output approximation overlaid on the data
figure %[output:26692fec]
rsp = plot(tAllData,fAllData); %[output:26692fec]
ax1 = rsp.Parent;
ShowRiemRect(ax1,double(fData),tData,EndPt); %[output:26692fec]
%[text] Notice that many of these rectangles are overestimating (or underestimating) the value of the signed area over that slice of interval. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B04568AE] ## Visualizing Error in Riemann Sum Approximations
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_E6A8072F] Visualizing the error at each step requires tracking how much over-estimation and under-estimation contributes to each estimate. The red area visualizes the overestimation of energy production, while green area visualizes the underestimation of energy production.
if ~exist("ax1","var")
    disp("Setting up default values...")
    syms t
    f(t) = sin(t);
    a = -pi;
    b = pi;
end
% Select the Riemann sum method of value approximation
EndPt = "left"; %[control:dropdown:7b4f]{"position":[9,15]}
  %[control:button:9ccd]{"position":[1,2]}
for Intervals = [1:10 12:2:24 25:5:100] %[output:group:845cfd3e]
    % Create plots as individual frames showing how increasing the 
    % number of intervals decreases the step size and impacts the estimate 
    % of total area
    tData = linspace(a,b,Intervals+1);
    tAllData = linspace(a,b,ceil(2000/Intervals)*Intervals+1);  % domain for plotting
    fAllData = double(f(tAllData));         % range for plotting
    fData = fAllData(1:ceil(2000/Intervals):end);
    tFine = reshape(tAllData(1:end-1),[],Intervals);
    tFine = [tFine; tData(2:end)]; %#ok<*AGROW> 
    fFine = reshape(fAllData(1:end-1),[],Intervals);
    fFine = [fFine; fData(2:end)];
    rSE = plot(tAllData,fAllData); %[output:663d48de]
    axRSA = rSE.Parent;
    if EndPt == "random"
        RepPts = tData(1:end-1) + diff(tData).*rand([1 Intervals]);
        fData = [fData f(RepPts)];
        tData = [tData RepPts];
    elseif EndPt == "midpoint"
        fData = [fData f((tData(2:end)+tData(1:end-1))/2)];
    elseif EndPt == "maximum"
        fMaxHelp = reshape(fAllData(1:end-1),[],Intervals);
        fMaxHelp = [fMaxHelp; fData(2:end)];
        fData = [fData max(fMaxHelp)];
    elseif EndPt == "minimum"
        fMinHelp = reshape(fAllData(1:end-1),[],Intervals);
        fMinHelp = [fMinHelp; fData(2:end)];
        fData = [fData min(fMinHelp)];
    end
    ShowRiemRect(axRSA,double(fData),tData,EndPt,1,tFine,double(fFine));
    WaitTime = 0.1; %[control:slider:3909]{"position":[16,19]}
    pause(WaitTime);
    drawnow
end %[output:group:845cfd3e]
%[text] ![](text:image:1832) **Try**.
%[text] 1. Change the [method of approximation](internal:M_E6A8072F) to right endpoint, midpoint, maximum value, and minimum value.
%[text] 2. Change the [function](internal:M_CD29503B) that you are studying.
%[text] 3. Use the [slider](internal:M_E6A8072F) or watch the animation to observe the impact of increasing/decreasing the step size. \
%[text] ![](text:image:9c9a) **Reflect**.
%[text] 1. What do you notice about the error for the different approximation methods?
%[text] 2. What impact does shortening the step size have on the total energy estimate? Does it matter which type of estimate you are using? Why or why not? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_20526209] ## Riemann Sum with Trapezoidal Approximation
%[text] Constant value approximations are frequently clearly bad approximations, even to the naked eye. Continuing the theme of approximating functions by polynomials, after a constant (0th degree polynomial) the 1st degree polynomial approximation is a line. Using the endpoints of each interval this gives us a trapezoidal approximation of the area under the curve.
syms x
f(x) =  (x-5)*(x+2)*(x-7); %[control:editfield:9c42]{"position":[8,26]}
StepSize = 170; %[control:slider:41b3]{"position":[12,15]}
xVals = -5:.01:5;
fVals = f(xVals);
p = plot(xVals,fVals); %[output:17bce09c]
ax = p.Parent;
% fplot(f(x),[1 10],"k-","LineWidth",2)
hold on %[output:17bce09c]
DisplayTrapezoidMethod(ax,xVals,double(fVals),1,length(xVals),StepSize,.01); %[output:17bce09c]
title("Trapezoidal Method with step size "+StepSize*(xVals(2)-xVals(1))) %[output:17bce09c]
subtitle("") %[output:17bce09c]
hold off %[output:17bce09c]
  %[control:button:6fe0]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3D8ED0F6] ### Trapezoidal Approximations with Error
%[text] Animate using a trapezoidal approximation with decreasing step sizes and compare the result to the exact area.
% Compute and display the energy with varying step sizes from 1 hour down
% to 5 minutes using the trapezoidal approximation
if ~exist("ax","var")
    xVals = -5:.01:5;
    fVals = (xVals-5).*(xVals+2).*(xVals-7);   % Set up the default values
    p = plot(xVals,fVals);
    ax = p.Parent;
    syms x
    f(x) = (x-5)*(x+2)*(x-7);
end
SumTot = DisplayTrapezoidMethod(ax,xVals,double(fVals),1,length(xVals),[200:-5:5 1],0.01); %[output:99710f69]
% Compute the best available energy estimate given the data
ExactTot = double(int(@(x)f(x),x,xVals(1),xVals(end)));
% Create a new plot of the estimates of energy generation against step size
plot([2:-.05:.05 0.01],SumTot) %[output:45278864]
hold on %[output:45278864]
% Add a line for the best estimate
cmap = colororder;
yline(ExactTot,":",Color=cmap(2,:)) %[output:45278864]
title("Area Estimates compared to exact value") %[output:45278864]
xlabel("Step size") %[output:45278864]
ylabel("Area") %[output:45278864]
legend(["Area Estimate","Exact Value"],"Location","best") %[output:45278864]
hold off %[output:45278864]
  %[control:button:2610]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
